description.narrative:
The intervention was implemented in a year that was affected by COVID pandemic and major political changes (new elected Parliament and new appointed Government). The partner followed the Activity Plan and adjusted the intervention according to the pandemic conditions. The following results are important to be mentioned:  

Component 1. Support the forensic institution reform

- the assessment was conducted on the collaboration and coordination within the national forensic institutions system and review of the current career development system for judicial experts (admission to the profession, initial and continuous training, qualification/licensing, performance evaluation, professional conduct, and disciplinary proceedings, etc.); 

- the Project continued its support towards enhancing capacities of national forensic institutions personnel in better communicating with the beneficiaries of the provided services as well as the general public.during the reporting period, the Project carried out training activities on the following subject areas: communication skills, public speaking, stress management, emotional intelligence, and crisis management trainings. three video tours of the national forensic institutions have been produced with the A2J Project support, describing the scope of work, mandate of the institutions and also providing a presentation of the laboratories and the necessary equipment for performing different types of judicial expertise.Building on the findings of the assessment of communications and outreach needs of the management and personnel of forensic institutions, the project worked towards unifying the visual identity of the institutions, by supporting the institutions to review the design of the existing institutional logos and developing new logos alongside with the internal and external Logo Usage Guidelines; 

- UNDP completed the assessment of deficiencies of interaction of forensic institutions with the demanders of forensic services. To identify gaps and opportunities for improving these relations a survey with the participation of judicial experts, judges, prosecutors, lawyers, and criminal investigation officers from different regions of the country was carried during the reporting period. In total 150 justice professionals from the project intervention regions participated the survey;

- UNDP alongside the Project Responsible Party IRP, worked to develop the Guide on judicial expertise for justice professionals, which aims at addressing the lack of information on scope and procedures associated with conducting a judicial expertise, noted in the assessment of deficiencies of interaction of forensic institutions with the demanders of forensic services;

- the findings and recommendations of the analysis of the legislative framework conducted with Project support during the reported period, as well proposals for amendments, have been shared with the national forensic institutions and Ministry of Justice and have been used in the process of review of the Order of the Minister of Justice no. 120/2018 on the approval of the Regulation of the Commission for Discipline of Judicial Experts and Order of the Minister of Justice no. 444/2017 on the approval of the Regulation on the procedure and criteria for evaluating the performance of judicial experts and the manner of awarding qualifications;

- the Functional analysis of the national forensic system was deployed and aims to provide a comprehensive review of the functionality of the national system of forensic institutions in the Republic of Moldova, both at the institutional and systemic levels;

- UNDP engaged with the three forensic institutions to work towards the set priorities in addressing the weaknesses pointed out in the findings of the Assessment of the institutional development needs. The following results were produced: 

- the Project continued with the support to the CLM Toxicology Unit in obtaining accreditation to SM SR EN ISO/IEC 17025:2017;

-  CLM was supported in its efforts towards ISO 9001:2015 Quality management systems10 certification, obtained in December 2021; 

- the Project supported capacity building of the personnel of the three national forensic institutions in the context of transition to the new version of ISO 17025 standard, ISO 9001 and ISO 17020;

- the Project completed drafting the Technical Concept of the Forensic Case Management System (FCMS), and supporting documentation, including the Regulatory Impact Assessment necessary for approval of normative acts which foresee the use of state budget, required for submitting the Concept for approval of the Government and submitted these to the Ministry of Justice;

- UNDP and the WLC developed and published a practice-based Standard Operating Procedure13 on forensic expertise in cases of domestic violence, which aims to enhance the efficiency of forensic doctors in their response to cases of domestic violence.describes the procedure to be followed when conducting forensic medical, psychiatric and clinical-psychological interventions /procedures in relation to domestic violence victims;

- the gender screening of five policies and regulations of Centre for Legal Medicine (5 documents revised out of which 2 approved) was carried out in 2021;

- a Training manual for forensic doctors intervention in domestic violence cases and learning support have been developed. The manual explains the domestic violence phenomenon, the international and national legal framework in the field, the health systems response, the measures, and actions necessary to prevent and combat domestic violence, causes and consequences of domestic violence and will be used by the lecturers of the Forensic Medicine Department of the Medicine and Farmacy University of Republic of Moldova;

- during the reporting period the baseline assessment of the coordinated response of the justice system to cases of domestic violence was completed. The current problems and barriers in the criminal justice system were identified and recommendations for implementing a more efficient and coordinated response of justice sector professionals to cases of domestic violence were provided.

Component 2. Coordination with justice professionals at the local level

- UNDP continued its work alongside the Project Responsible Party, IRP, its work in the four target areas (Soroca, Criuleni, Cimislia, Comrat) to facilitate the work of Dialogue Platforms on Access to Justice (Dialogue Platforms) composed of justice chain actors;

- the Dialogue Platforms composition was extended in 2021, from 73 members to 78, as representatives of the justice chain institutions from Dubasari district expressed interest in joining the discussions in the Criuleni platform and provide their inputs in relation to barriers faced by population on the left bank such as: failure to recognize documents and decisions issued by unconstitutional authorities (ex. in judicial cases related to determination of the alimony, childs domicile, inheritance where the documents that were issued by Transnistrian institutions and presented by the parties in the judicial proceedings are not admitted as relevant evidence by the national courts (ex. Criuleni, Grigoropol etc.).

- the members of all four platforms addressed the National Legal Aid Council (NLAC) with the formal request to increase the number of lawyers offering qualified state guaranteed legal aid and of paralegals as well as recommending identification of a solution/mechanism which would allow provision of primary legal state guaranteed legal aid services by NGOs that have programs and experience in providing free primary legal aid to people from vulnerable or marginalised groups. As a result, in 2021 the NLAC territorial offices were supplemented by 5 paralegals and 70 lawyers for delivering state guaranteed legal aid;

- the members of the Dialogue Platforms also enhanced their knowledge and skills as part of the capacity building and professional development program on the following subject areas: addressing systemic issues in the field of access to justice, referral of cases to public and/or civil society institutions in order to solve socio-legal problems of the beneficiaries, effective tactics in communicating with beneficiaries/litigants, communication and interaction with the public, including media, access to justice for minority groups (linguistic, ethnic, religious) and people with disabilities;

- 4 local mini-initiatives submitted by legal professionals from supported regions were approved to be financed: 

1. Strengthen the multidisciplinary approach to ensuring a life free of violence: 3 training sessions during which 35 persons from 16 localities of Criuleni district were informed about Gender Based Violence organised;
2. Strengthening access to justice within the Cimilia prosecutor's office: A special hearing room, with the possibility of audio and video recording, for criminal trial participants, created at the Cimislia Prosecutor's Office. Hence, high quality standards are applied in dealing with criminal cases in which hearings are conducted. A space of 38.5 m2 has been adapted. It is protected by a mirror wall, to allow the recognition of suspects in safe and confidential conditions. The necessary space was provided by the Cimislia Prosecutor's Office. The room has also been soundproofed and endowed with modern equipment, such as video cameras and microphones;
3. Increased access for people with locomotor deficiencies in the building of the local court from Soroca: an access ramp installed at the entrance to the Soroca District Court headquarters, to facilitate access to justice for people with locomotor disabilities, in equal and non-discriminatory manner. The intervention was coordinated closely with the Center for the Rights of Persons with Disabilities as to ensure that the construction complies with technical accessibility regulations for people with locomotor disabilities, namely: the ramp is made of non-slip material, the inclination angle of the ramp is according to accessibility standards, and bars are installed on both sides of the ramp. support at an appropriate distance.
4. Increased access to informational resources for the local court from Dubasari, Criuleni: At the initiative of two judges, a legal library with literature in Romanian was opened. This will help to improve the knowledge and skills of justice professionals and increase the quality and efficiency of justice. Moreover, the legal library, open to all interested people, serves as a source of information for citizens in the security zone.

- during the reporting period, UNDP and Invento organised an online legal hackathon35 -HackJustice: An Access to Justice Hackathon;

-  UNDP and National Legal Aid Council worked on raising the awareness of population in the project intervention regions on the new functionalities of the Information System CNAJGS (IS CNAJGS);

- the institutional capacities building program for the four local NGOs-beneficiaries of the Project conceptualized based on the institutional needs assessment of the NGOs conducted by IRP during the Inception Phase was initiated and implemented at 80 %;

- the NGOs recipients of the institutional support grants started the implementation of the projects described in the grant agreements:

1. NGO Each one Contributes to a Change (Criuleni): Elaboration and piloting of the Regulation on the identification, assistance and referral of cases for women and men with disabilities and/or subjected to domestic violence in Criuleni and Dubasari districts in the field of access to justice

2. NGO Youth Resource Center DaciaCounselling: a referral service for young people in the field of access to justice was created; Report on "Access to justice for young people in Soroca district" elaborated and it will be publicly discussed in the region.

3. NGO Association Legal Clinic (Comrat): conducted public lectures on human rights thematic in different settlements of ATU Gagauzia; monitoring on access to justice for people from social vulnerable groups in Gagauzia conducted and report elaborated;

4. Association Local Democracy Agency of Moldova (Cimislia): elaborated a Report Access to justice of vulnerable and marginalized groups from Cimislia.

- the Project initiated the support to the National Bureau of Statistics (NBS), in carrying out the sectoral evaluation of official statistics on crime and justice in the Republic of Moldova in line with European Standards and United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC).The sectoral evaluation of official statistics in the field of crime and justice will address central public authorities, the police, the judiciary, the penitentiary, the prosecutor's office, as well as representatives of other law enforcement agencies from the country and aims to determine the degree of compliance of crime and justice statistics in the Republic of Moldova with the international standards in the field, in particular of the Manual for the Development of a system of Criminal Justice Statistics;

- a Guide for the Beneficiary containing schematically depicted information and leaflets were developed and distributed through the National Legal Aid Council network;

- the Project also carried out a campaign to promote the mandate and activities of the Dialogue Platforms on Access to Justice which reached 81,250 citizens;

- 28 school pupils from four districts of the country, gathered in a Youth Summer Camp, learnt what human rights are, which institutions are responsible for their protection, and what tools are at national and international level for the protection of human rights.

Update 2024:
Sida extended the project til December 2024. The amendment represents a consolidation phase of the project initiated for the period of 2020-2023 and will extend till December 2024. The additionally requested funds will be used to consolidate and enhance sustainability of the results and activities achieved under the ongoing project, in line with the work plan here attached for the consolidation phase.
The additional activities will lead to an accredited Centre for Legal Medicine (accredited in line with ISO/IEC 17025:2017 (biology department) and ISO/IEC 17020:2012) and National Center of Judicial Expertise and Police Forensic Center accredited/certified in line with ISO/IEC 17020:2012. In addition, the support will focus on aamendments to the regulatory and policy framework in forensic area. The new Phase will also enable for the Forensic Case Management System (FCMS) to become operational through transmitting the ownership of the system toward Ministry of Justice.
More specifically, additional activities will support the set project Outputs as follows:

Output 1: Forensic system strengthened with digitalised processes and sustainable technical, professional and administrative capacities
- Enhance the coordination and collaboration among the forensic institutions:
Support to enhancing collaboration among the forensic institutions and experts, as well as implementation of change management practices in their strategic activities directed to the transposition of the changes to the normative framework regulating the judicial expertise area by engaging them in regular subject matter dialogues and events, common capacity and team building exercises, annual conferences; Strengthen international cooperation and exchange with peer institutions and professional networks from EU member states to align to the EU directives.
- Strengthen institutional and professional performance of the forensic system:
Support the Centre for Legal Medicine in setting up the quality management system and comply with the requirements of ISO accreditation. A technical assessment of the existing wastewater sewage system within the Centre for Legal Medicine will be carried out and a feasibility study on the possible solutions for equipping CLM with modern and environment compliant facilities for wastewater sewage system will be developed.
The operationalization of environmental expertise within the National Centre of Judicial Expertise will be supported by equipping the laboratory with the necessary basic equipment, kits and/or consumables as well training of personnel.
Training for forensic doctors and auxiliary medical staff on the revised Regulation governing the determination of bodily injuries, with an emphasis on psychological violence, as well as communicating in a sensitive manner and focused on the needs of the victim.
- Development of Forensic Case Management System (e-system):
Support to creation of the hardware and software environments, provision of strategic advice, support services and ICT capacity building to forensic institutions, the Ministry of Justice of the Republic of Moldova and Agency for Legal Informational Resources necessary for enabling the proper functioning and use of the FCMS and interoperability of data used and generated by the system.

Output 2: Enhanced capacities for coordinated justice delivery and improved access to justice for the vulnerable groups at the subnational level
Strengthen the knowledge and research base for sector wide collaboration and interventions. The Project will support the National Bureau of Statistics in implementation of the Roadmap to operationalize the recommendations of the Assessment of crime and justice statistics carried out in 2022. Additionally, a review of the legal framework and the national practice in the area of technology-facilitated gender-based violence will be carried out and recommendations for future amendments to the normative framework will be developed.

Update 2024 results:
Over the course of the project, and particularly during the consolidation phase in 2024, a number of key results have been achieved, both expected deliverables aligned with the project design, and unanticipated outcomes that reflect the project's adaptive approach to Moldovas evolving justice context.

One of the most significant expected results was the advancement of the Forensic Case Management System (FCMS). After initial delays in 2023, the project successfully transitioned into the piloting and testing phases in 2024. The systems technical specifications, workflows, and interface were developed and validated in collaboration with all three national forensic institutions. Although performance testing revealed some infrastructure limitations, particularly when handling high user loads, the system architecture proved scalable, and technical adjustments were made to address identified issues. This progress puts the FCMS on track for full operational deployment in early 2025.

Another important milestone was the progress toward ISO/IEC accreditation for Moldovas forensic institutions. The project facilitated the preparation of the Forensic Biology Department at the Centre for Legal Medicine (CLM) for ISO/IEC 17025:2018 accreditation and supported the Police Forensic Centres efforts toward ISO/IEC 17020:2012. These efforts included technical assessments, tailored training, and the development of standard operating procedures (SOPs) a crucial step toward ensuring the scientific credibility and quality assurance of forensic outputs.

In parallel, the project supported the revision of Moldovas legal and regulatory framework for judicial expertise. While legislative progress was slower than expected in 2023, 2024 saw the Ministry of Justice take greater ownership of the reform process. Draft amendments covering the recognition of experts, flexible scheduling for urgent examinations, and transparent qualification procedures were developed and endorsed at the technical level, marking a shift toward institutional alignment and reform readiness.

On the access to justice side, 2024 saw the reactivation of regional Dialogue Platforms in Soroca, Criuleni, Cimislia, and Comrat. These forums brought together local justice actors to identify systemic barriers and co-create practical solutions. Their work culminated in a national event and a set of joint recommendations, including proposals for mobile legal aid teams, expanded paralegal networks, and tailored legal education programmes results that aligned closely with the projects goals under Output 2, which had been inactive in 2023.

The project also successfully developed and disseminated awareness materials to promote mediation services. Using animated videos and dialogue-based storytelling, the campaign demystified the mediation process and promoted it as a practical, accessible alternative to formal litigationparticularly important for vulnerable populations. These multimedia tools marked an effective communication strategy and will continue to be disseminated during the final months of the project.

In addition to these planned outcomes, several unexpected but positive results emerged. For example, the projects support to the NGO Eco Contact led to the first-ever technical and environmental assessment of the wastewater infrastructure at the Centre for Legal Medicinean overlooked but critical issue for ensuring compliance with environmental and public health standards. This initiative laid the foundation for future infrastructure upgrades in the medico-legal sector.

Another unanticipated outcome came from the collaboration with the Womens Law Centre, which led not only to improved training for forensic doctors on handling gender-based violence (GBV) cases, but also to the development of legislative proposals aimed at aligning Moldovas domestic legislation with the Istanbul Convention. These amendments addressed digital forms of violence and proposed changes to over 20 legal instrumentsextending the projects impact beyond technical training into national legal reform.

Finally, the project contributed to Moldovas broader statistical and institutional alignment with EU standards through support for the implementation of the International Classification of Crime for Statistical Purposes (ICCS). This work included multi-agency workshops and the development of draft statistical correspondence tables, further positioning Moldova to generate reliable, internationally comparable crime and justice data.
